.TH ASB 1 "2026" "asb" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
asb \- run AI coding agents in kernel-enforced sandboxes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B asb
[\fB\-s\fR \fIsandbox\fR] [\fB\-y\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B asb
runs AI coding agents (Claude Code, Hermes, ...) as dedicated locked-down
OS accounts, called
.IR sandboxes .
Project access is granted per directory with POSIX ACLs, so the kernel
\(em not application configuration \(em enforces what an agent can read
or write. An agent inside a sandbox cannot reach your home directory,
your credentials, or other projects.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B create \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIagent\fR]
Create a sandbox: a dedicated OS account with a private home, plus a
scoped passwordless sudoers rule so you can switch into it. The first
sandbox created becomes the default.
.TP
.B delete \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-purge\fR]
Delete a sandbox's sudoers rule and configuration. With
.BR \-\-purge ,
also remove its OS account and home directory.
.TP
.B use \fIname\fR
Set the default sandbox.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fIname\fR]
Show sandboxes, their default agents, and their granted directories.
.TP
.B add \fR[\fIdir\fR]
Grant the sandbox read/write access to a directory (default: the current
directory). Tightens the directory against other users, applies ACLs with
inheritance, adds traverse-only permission on parent directories, and
warns if a world-readable parent would leak sibling names.
.TP
.B remove \fR[\fIdir\fR]
Revoke a grant.
.TP
.B recover
Re-apply every grant from configuration. Useful after restoring from a
backup tool or sync service that does not preserve ACLs.
.TP
.B launch \fR[\fIsandbox\fR] [\fIagent\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIargs\fR]
Launch an agent in the current directory, inside a sandbox. With no
arguments, uses the default sandbox and that sandbox's default agent.
Refuses to run unless the current directory has been granted. Extra
arguments after \fB\-\-\fR pass through to the agent.
.TP
.B lockdown
One-off hardening of your own account: remove group/other permissions
from everything under \fB$HOME\fR and set \fBumask 077\fR for new files.
.TP
.B doctor
Check that ACLs, sudoers rules, and agents are working.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sandbox " " \fIname\fR
Operate on this sandbox instead of the default.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Assume yes on confirmation prompts.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/asb/config
Global settings (default_sandbox, default_agent).
.TP
.I ~/.config/asb/agents.conf
Agent definitions, one per line: \fIname\fR=\fIcommand line\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.config/asb/<sandbox>/directories
Granted directories for a sandbox, one absolute path per line.
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/asb\-<sandbox>
The scoped passwordless switching rule.
.SH CAVEATS
The sandbox boundary is standard Unix user separation: it does not
provide network isolation, it does not protect secrets inside granted
directories, and it is not a defense against kernel privilege-escalation
exploits. Sync services such as Dropbox do not preserve POSIX ACLs; run
.B asb recover
if grants are lost.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setfacl (1),
.BR getfacl (1),
.BR sudoers (5)
